rt of the system and that's  geometry consider a vertical plane  that's being illuminated by a big light  source now this light source is giving  out a certain amount of energy per  square meter let's say a hundred watts  per square meter and because our plane  is parallel to it it's receiving an  uniform amount of energy across it a  hundred watts per square meter  now if the earth was a cube like a  Minecraft world then the side facing the  Sun at any given time would be receiving  the same life  radiation at every point and so would be  at the same temperature but the earth  isn't a cube it's a sphere and so it's  surface is curved that means that if the  earth's equator is parallel to our light  source the Sun then areas towards the  poles are tilted with respect to it and  we can examine what that means by going  back to our plane and tilting it with  respect to our light source doing so we  can see that the plane now receives less  energy in total because it occupies a  smaller area from the perspective of the  light source an absorbing less energy in  total means that less energy is being  absorbed per square meter and that means  that the plane will equilibrate at a  lower temperature because less energy is  coming in that means that less energy  needs to be emitted thermally for the  plane to be an equilibrium and it can do  that at a lower temperature the greater  the angle between the light source and  the plane the less energy is incident on  the plane and so the lower the  temperature the plane will thermally  equilibrate at and because the poles are  very high angles to the Sun nearly 90  degrees they receive very little energy  and so they're very cold in fact the  poles will be even colder if it weren't  for the atmosphere to see how much of an  impact it has we can look at the moon  which receives about the same energy per  square meter  as we do but has basically no atmosphere  at all and on the moon we see about a  300 degree Celsius difference between  the temperatures are the pole and the  equator at midday now compare that to  the 70 to 80 degrees difference that we  see here on earth this is because the  atmosphere absorbs some heat and  redistributes over the surface of the  earth and so reduces the temperature  difference between the equator and the  poles as well as reducing the  temperature difference between night and  day the atmosphere is pretty useful the  next component in talking about the  stratospheric polar vortex what is a  vortex a vortex can be defined as a  region in a fluid where the flow is  primarily around an axis line or as I  tend to think of it a spinny thing a  vortex is how a physicist or a fluid  analysis would describe chunk of  spinning or road  in fluid or air while a meteorologist  would look at the same phenomenon and  describe it as a cyclone or an  anticyclone depending on which way  around the rotation is if the air is  spinning in the same direction as the  Earth's rotation then it's a cyclone or  is if the air is spinning in the  opposite direction to the Earth's  rotation then it's an anticyclone that  then means that if you're in the  northern hemisphere looking for the top  down  a cyclone rotates anti-clockwise while  an anticyclone rotates clockwise  slightly confusing but in the southern  hemisphere  it's the other way around yay for the  southern hemisphere the most common way  that cyclones form is due to a pressure  gradient an area of low pressure on the  Earth's surface the air is drawn into  and due to the Earth's rotation starts  to spin around now that's a topic that  we could cover in a lot more detail a  future course on meteorology but for now  all you need to know is that cyclones  are associated with areas of low  pressure now going back to the second  video again we talked about the ideal  gas equation which showed us the  relationship between the pressure  temperature and density of air and using  this in combination with what we just  talked about the fact that the Earth's  poles are very cold and making the  assumption that the density of air  across the surface of the earth is about  the same everywhere we can see that by  the poles being very cold the air here  is at a lower pressure than elsewhere  and that means a big old pressure  gradient which means a big old cyclone  otherwise known as a vortex a polar  vortex but to explain the stratospheric  polar vortex this isn't enough there's  another piece of physics involved the  mathematics of the thermal wind goes a  fair way beyond the scope of this course  but I wanted to include it just to be a  little bit more complete and the gist of  it is this if you have a horizontal  temperature gradient so two locations at  the same height having different  temperatures then winds which are  perpendicular to that temperature  gradient become stronger and stronger  the higher and higher up you go in the  atmosphere I'm probably gonna need to  explain that a little bit more what this  means is that by the equator being  warmer than the poles winds which blow  perpendicular for that temperature  gradient so from west to east if they  were blowing from north to south and  they be parallel to that temperature  gradient become stronger and stronger  the higher you look in the app  so in the stratosphere for example winds  get intensified by that temperature  gradient more than winds in the  troposphere below it do  so really thermal wind is a bit of a  misnomer what we're really talking about  here is a wind shear and the most famous  example of it is the jet streams which  in the mid-latitudes and exist roughly  between the stratosphere and the  troposphere and are these narrow bands  of really intense winds but what we're  talking about is even higher up in the  atmosphere because the other famous  example of the thermal wind is the  intensification of the stratospheric  polar vortex so let's finally look at it  a combination of the low pressures over  the pole and 