pped air over the pole can get  incredibly cold now what we haven't  talked about is how the strength of the  vortex varies over time because various  factors ranging from relatively simple  ones like the temperature difference  between the equator and the pole to  relatively complex ones like variations  in ep flux divergence due to changes in  planetary wave refraction ya mean the  the strength of the vortex varies over  time and variations in the vortex  strength means variations in how well  the ultra cold arctic air is kept  constrained to higher latitudes should  the polar vortex become very weak or  even be displaced from the pole then  that freezing air would spill further  south and bring freezing temperatures  and ice and snow and chaos with it so  it's just as well that doesn't happen  now I'm just kidding it happens a lot  and we're going to talk about those  events in the next video so in this  video we've talked about radiation  balance and thermodynamic equilibrium  and how it explains why the Earth's  poles are colder than the equator  because they receive less so in a  radiation due to the geometry of a  curved planet we've talked about what a  vortex is it being a spinny thing we've  talked about how the stratospheric polar  vortex is intensified by the thermal  wind and we've started to consider just  why the polar vortex might be important  next time we'll continue this discussion  and talk about the events called a  sudden stratospheric warming as the most  violent events in the stratosphere which  can have a significant impact on our  weather here at the surface as well as  being what I study for my PhD  thank you for watching if you enjoyed  this video and you'd like to support it  then you can do so on my Connors page  and if you did enjoy it then please  consider liking it and subscribe to this  channel for more educational content the  important thing is that if the a/o index  is negative and the polar vortex is  weaker than normal and so t